      python3 book_demo.py 3            (prints chapter 3 only)
      python3 book_demo.py search WORD  (finds WORD across chapters)
      python3 book_demo.py html         (writes book.html)
"""
import json, sys, html as _h

BOOK = {'title': 'Murder at Hollyhock Lane', 'author': 'Signature original', 'genre': 'Tearoom Mysteries', 'chapters': [{'n': 1, 'title': 'Tea and Tidings', 'text': "The morning Iris Meadows found a pressed flower on the doorstep of her cottage in Hollyhock Lane, the kettle had just begun to sing. She turned the object over in her hands, frowning, for it plainly belonged to someone — and someone in Hollyhock Lane was missing it badly.\n\nThat evening Iris Meadows spread her notes on the parlor table: a map of Hollyhock Lane, a list of names, and a pressed flower in the center like a spider. 'Someone,' she said to the cat, 'is lying. And I mean to find out who.'\n\nBefore bed she wrote in her journal: Day one. The game is on. Tomorrow I walk the hedgerows and ask the questions no one else will ask. the kindness of neighbors — that is what this is about, though no one says so yet."}, {'n': 2, 'title': 'The Missing Piece', 'text': "At the riverside inn, the landlord remembered the jealous heir drinking alone, turning a pressed flower over and over. 'Nervous hands,' he said. 'Guilty hands, if you ask me.' Iris Meadows asked him, and wrote it down.\n\nIris Meadows began with Hollyhock Lane itself, walking every lane with a pressed flower wrapped in a handkerchief. The hedgerows of Hollyhock Lane had seen everything, she always said; one had only to ask the right people in the right order.\n\nConstable Henry Bly brought the parish records, and together they found the gap: a name missing where a name should be, a date that did not fit. a pressed flower was the key, Iris Meadows was certain; it unlocked a story someone had paid to keep shut."}, {'n': 3, 'title': 'Questions at Dusk', 'text': 'Constable Henry Bly kept watch from the garden gate while Iris Meadows asked about a pressed flower. the jealous heir claimed never to have seen it — but described it perfectly, down to the scratch on its side. Iris Meadows noted that, too.\n\nBut Iris Meadows shook her head. Something was missing — a motive with a human heart in it. a pressed flower was not mere property; it was a promise, or a debt, or a love. She would find which before she accused anyone.\n\nAt dusk she walked to the old vicarage alone, turning a pressed flower in her pocket. The answer, she felt, was close — as close as the church bell that began to toll, counting out the hours like clues.'}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Storm Breaks', 'text': "They went out in rain capes, lanterns swinging, and found the disturbed earth — and beneath it, not treasure, but proof: letters, a photograph, and the mate to a pressed flower. The whole sad story lay in the mud.\n\nthe jealous heir appeared then, wild-eyed, and confessed before Iris Meadows could speak: it had begun as a kindness, grown into a theft, and ended in a lie that poisoned Hollyhock Lane. 'I never meant —' began the jealous heir, and could not finish.\n\nThe storm that had threatened Hollyhock Lane all week broke at last, rattling the windows of Hollyhock Lane. In the midst of it came a hammering at Iris Meadows's door: Constable Henry Bly, soaked, with news — the jealous heir had been seen digging near the old vicarage."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "a pressed flower was restored to its rightful keeper, who wept to hold it. Iris Meadows declined all thanks, as was her custom, and slipped away before the speeches. The case was closed; the village could breathe.\n\nOn market day Iris Meadows laid it all before Hollyhock Lane: a pressed flower, the letters, the photograph, the confession. The crowd in the square was silent as a church. the jealous heir stood with head bowed and did not deny a word.\n\nthe jealous heir's motive, when all heard it, drew more pity than anger — a lost inheritance, a sick child, a desperate choice. Hollyhock Lane was stern, but Hollyhock Lane was also kind; and kindness found a way to help where the law could only punish."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Quiet After', 'text': 'And if another mystery comes to Hollyhock Lane — as mysteries do, like weather — Iris Meadows will be ready, kettle on, journal open. For the kindness of neighbors, always, in Hollyhock Lane and everywhere.\n\nA week later Hollyhock Lane was itself again: the fete committee argued about bunting, the bakery smelled of scones, and a pressed flower sat in its proper place, no longer a mystery but a keepsake with a story.\n\nthe jealous heir, from wherever the law had sent him, wrote a letter of thanks: for the truth, for the pity, for the chance to begin again. Iris Meadows read it twice and filed it with the case notes.'}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

