tters, the photograph, the confession. The crowd in the square was silent as a church. the jealous heir stood with head bowed and did not deny a word."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Quiet After', 'text': "A week later the market town of Bramblewick was itself again: the fete committee argued about bunting, the bakery smelled of scones, and a missing will sat in its proper place, no longer a mystery but a keepsake with a story.\n\nthe jealous heir, from wherever the law had sent him, wrote a letter of thanks: for the truth, for the pity, for the chance to begin again. Edith Plum read it twice and filed it with the case notes.\n\nDr. Samuel Reed brought flowers — 'for the best detective in the county,' they said, gruff as ever. Edith Plum put them in water and told them not to be silly, but she kept the card."}]}

